<article class="env-remark" id="03E6">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03E6">Remark <span data-tag="03E6">11.5.0.56</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a data-tag="02TG" href="/tag/02TG">7.4.4.12</a>, suppose that $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a left fibration. Then the extension $\overline{U}: \overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleleft }$ is also a left fibration. To prove this, it will suffice to show that the fiber $\overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}}_{ {\bf 0} }$ is a Kan complex (Proposition <a data-tag="01UM" href="/tag/01UM">5.1.4.15</a>). This follows from the fact that the covariant diffraction functor </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \mathrm{Df}: \overline{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}}_{ {\bf 0} } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}_{/ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}^{\operatorname{CCart}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}) = \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}) \]
</div>
<p> is an equivalence of $\infty $-categories, since the simplicial set $\operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{E}})$ is a Kan complex by (Corollary <a data-tag="01GZ" href="/tag/01GZ">4.4.2.5</a>). </p>
</article>
